.\" zvolfiles(1) — manual page, mdoc. Kept true to the program by
.\" tests/man-check.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ZVOLFILES 1
.Os zvolrescue 0.9.15
.Sh NAME
.Nm zvolfiles
.Nd files out of ZFS filesystem datasets, without importing the pool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm list
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm extract
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm objects
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a filesystem dataset of a ZFS pool at any transaction group that
still verifies and writes its files out: directories, files, and
symbolic links as files holding their targets, with their times, modes
and, as root, owners, each file's blocks verified by their checksums.
When the POSIX metadata is too damaged for a tree,
.Cm objects
writes one file per object instead.
Nothing is written to the members.
.Pp
The global options and the pool member options are those of
.Xr zvolrescue 1 :
.Fl f , Fl -format Ar text | json ,
.Fl v , Fl -verbose ,
.Fl q , Fl -quiet ,
.Fl -evidence-log ,
.Fl -hash-inputs ,
.Fl -no-color ,
.Fl -debug ,
.Fl -debug-log ,
and
.Fl -image ,
.Fl -hints ,
.Fl -search-order ,
.Fl -pool-guid ,
.Fl -assume-member ,
.Fl -ignore-unknown-features ,
.Fl -map ,
.Fl -device-may-fail ,
.Fl -surface-scan-on-device .
.Sh OPTIONS
Taken by every command:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -txg Ar n
Transaction group to read; the newest that verifies when absent.
.It Fl -key Ar keyspec
Dataset encryption key:
.Ql raw: Ns Ar file ,
.Ql hex: Ns Ar hex ,
.Ql passphrase: Ns Ar file ,
or
.Ql prompt .
A hex or passphrase file is read as
.Ql zfs load-key
reads one: its first line, without the newline that ends it.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss list
.Nm Cm list
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Op Ar options
.Pp
Show what is in the dataset.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -path Ar path
Path inside the dataset; the root when absent.
A flag rather than a trailing word: the member list is variable-length,
so a bare path after it could not be told from another member.
.It Fl R , Fl -recursive
Descend into subdirectories.
.El
.Ss extract
.Nm Cm extract
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Pp
Write the tree out as files.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar dir
Write the tree here.
Nothing already in
.Ar dir
is written through: a symbolic link in the way of a file or a
directory is removed and the entry made in its place, and a file in
the way of a file is replaced, as
.Xr tar 1
does.
The directory itself, and every path made in it, is refused with exit
5 if it is anything the run reads or shares bytes with it: a member
under any name, a map, the hints, a key file, a device a member is on.
An entry so refused is left as it is and named in the manifest; the
rest of the tree is still written.
An entry whose name no file can have (empty,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&.. ,
or holding
.Ql /
or NUL), or whose path another entry already took, is not written and
is named in the manifest with the reason; its object is still there for
.Cm objects .
When that entry is a directory, nothing under it is written either,
and each entry under it is named.
A directory met a second time \(em damaged metadata, since a directory
has one name \(em is named in the manifest and not walked again.
A fifo, a device or a socket is recorded in the manifest, with a
device's major and minor number, and not made; it is no failure.
The number is split as the system that made the node stored it \(em
the illumos form when its high 32 bits are set, the Linux form when
not \(em and the manifest says which
.Pq Li encoding
beside the raw value.
A block that cannot be read is written as zeros, and the manifest
says which bytes and why
.Pq Li zeroed ;
a leaf of a directory that cannot be read loses the entries in it and
no others, and is named on the directory's record
.Pq Li contents_errors .
The manifest records every file's four times to the nanosecond, before
1970 too, and any extended attribute it could not read.
The manifest is
.Pa manifest.json ,
or
.Pa manifest.1.json
and so on when the dataset has a file of that name at its root or an
earlier run left one there.
.It Fl -path Ar path
Path inside the dataset, repeatable; everything when absent.
It is taken as the dataset spells it: a name found by folding case or
by normalizing is written under the name on disk, and
.Ql \&./
is dropped.
The directory a path names is written with its own metadata, and a
path inside another one given is written once.
A path not in the dataset at that TXG is named, in the manifest too
.Pq Li not_found ,
and the others are written (exit 3); when none is there, nothing is
made.
.It Fl -strict
Stop the run at the first thing that cannot be read \(em a file's
block, an entry's metadata, a directory's entries, an extended
attribute: the entry it is in is named as failed, a file holding
exactly the bytes before it, and nothing after it is looked at (exit
4).
A file a signal cuts short holds exactly the bytes before the cut too.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
With
.Cm extract ,
name each entry the summary counts as not read whole or not written,
and why.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
With
.Cm extract ,
leave out the notes of what
.Fl -preserve
could not put back; the manifest has them.
.It Fl -preserve Ar list
Metadata to put back, comma-separated:
.Ql times
(modification and access times),
.Ql mode
(permission bits),
.Ql owner
(user and group, which needs root), or
.Ql none ;
default
.Ql times,mode .
Anything else is a usage error.
The set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits are restored only with the owner,
so that a program of the dataset does not become one that runs as
whoever extracted it.
A symbolic link is written as a file holding its target, mode 0644.
A directory gets its metadata after everything in it is written.
What the platform would not put back is said once and listed in the
manifest as
.Li not_restored ;
the manifest records what every file had either way.
Holes in a file stay holes.
.El
.Ss objects
.Nm Cm objects
.Ar dataset
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Pp
One file per object, for when the POSIX metadata is gone.
Only the object set has to be readable: a master node or system
attribute table that is not is said on standard error and in
.Pa objects.json ,
and the objects are written all the same.
The index is
.Pa objects.json ,
or
.Pa objects.1.json
and so on when an earlier run left one there.
A transaction group at which the dataset does not open is passed over
for an older one, by every subcommand.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar dir
Write the objects here, by the same rules as
.Cm extract .
.It Fl -strict
Stop the run at the first block that cannot be read (exit 4).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
As in
.Xr zvolrescue 1 :
0 success, 1 usage (a missing key among it), 2 evidence unreadable, 3
the dataset unreadable at the requested TXG, or a
.Fl -path
not in it there, 4 not every file whole, 5
refused, 6 interrupted by a signal, 7 the medium refused a read.
.Sh EXAMPLES
One directory of a home dataset, at the TXG before it was emptied:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zvolfiles list pool/home /dev/ada0p3 /dev/ada1p3 -R --txg 4816229
zvolfiles extract pool/home /dev/ada0p3 /dev/ada1p3 --txg 4816229 \e
    --path alice/thesis -o /mnt/rescue/home
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zvolcarve 1 ,
.Xr zvolreport 1 ,
.Xr zvolrescue 1 ,
.Xr zvoltimeline 1
